          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have had reflux for years and now food sometimes sticks going down. How worrying is that, and what does guidance describe about when to investigate?",
          "answer": "If symptoms are severe, worsening, or new and unexplained, seek medical advice. Difficulty swallowing food, especially in the context of long-standing reflux, is a symptom that warrants medical attention (diagnostic). This symptom is often referred to as dysphagia (diagnostic).\n\nGuidance states that for adults with typical reflux symptoms (heartburn and regurgitation) and no \"alarm features,\" the usual initial approach is an eight-week trial of acid suppression medication, rather than immediate investigation (diagnostic, guideline-gerd-initial-management-and-alarm-features; research, guideline-gerd-initial-management-and-alarm-features). The research specialist notes that this is because typical symptoms that improve with acid suppression are often informative enough, and endoscopy first would rarely change management in this group (research, guideline-gerd-initial-management-and-alarm-features).\n\nThe diagnostic specialist notes that difficulty swallowing is considered an \"alarm feature,\" which changes the management approach and indicates that investigation may be needed (diagnostic, guideline-gerd-initial-management-and-alarm-features). However, the research specialist indicates that the guidance does not specify what constitutes an \"alarm feature\" that would prompt investigation, especially for dysphagia (food sticking) (research, guideline-gerd-initial-management-and-alarm-features). The presence of esophagitis (inflammation of the esophagus) can only be determined through endoscopic findings, not from symptoms alone (diagnostic).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
